================================================================================
1. WHAT THE COMMIT CLAIMS
================================================================================

The log under review makes four factual claims. Everything else in it is a
consequence of them.

  C1. QEMU's `hda-duplex` codec has no internal DAC->ADC path: node 4 (ADC)
      connects only to node 5 (Line In); node 3 (Line Out) connects to node 2
      (DAC).
  C2. With `-audiodev none`, playback samples are discarded and capture returns
      silence (the log's words: "0 bytes / all 0x00s").
  C3. Therefore a true round trip is impossible in QEMU without host-side
      loopback (ALSA `snd-aloop`), and the commit stops there, as the work order
      permits ("If QEMU cannot give a true round trip, say so and stop").
  C4. `-audiodev wav` capture is forbidden by the invariant "Camera and audio
      bytes live in RAM only", so it is not an alternative.
  C5. Display: K2 already covers everything K5 needs (framebuffer 1024x768 @
      32bpp, pitch 4096, 95 printable ASCII glyphs, SeaBIOS and OVMF).

The whole commit is documentation. There is no code to break, so the two plants
below test (a) whether the one executable oracle the log leans on really can
fail, and (b) whether any repo tool guards the log's numbers and citations.

================================================================================
2. ORACLE: THE CLAIM IS CHECKED AGAINST QEMU'S OWN SOURCE, NOT THE KERNEL
================================================================================

QEMU is the external tool here. It is independent of the kernel driver under
review: the kernel in this repo never executes QEMU's C code, and QEMU predates
this work order. Fetched by commit hash, not by branch, so the citation can be
re-created exactly:

  QEMU commit f71afb409a87c4ecd0f9d63a5963eb645dfab780 (2026-11-05T15:24:53Z)
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qemu/qemu/f71afb409a87c4ecd0f9d63a5963eb645dfab780/hw/audio/hda-codec-common.h
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qemu/qemu/f71afb409a87c4ecd0f9d63a5963eb645dfab780/audio/noaudio.c
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qemu/qemu/f71afb409a87c4ecd0f9d63a5963eb645dfab780/hw/audio/hda-codec.c
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qemu/qemu/f71afb409a87c4ecd0f9d63a5963eb645dfab780/audio/wavaudio.c

C1, the duplex codec description. In hw/audio/hda-codec-common.h the
`duplex_nodes_` initialiser contains:

  $ grep -n "\.conn    = (uint32_t\[\]) {" hw_audio_hda-codec-common.h
  217:        .conn    = (uint32_t[]) { 2 },      <- output codec, node 3 (out)
  310:        .conn    = (uint32_t[]) { 2 },      <- duplex codec, node 3 (out)
  317:        .conn    = (uint32_t[]) { 5 },      <- duplex codec, node 4 (adc)
  421:        .conn    = (uint32_t[]) { 2 },      <- micro codec, node 3 (out)
  428:        .conn    = (uint32_t[]) { 5 },      <- micro codec, node 4 (adc)

Parsed mechanically, with a throwaway script in the scratch clone (not added to
the repo; reviews report, they do not add tests), so the answer comes from the
source text rather than from reading it by eye. The script isolates
`duplex_nodes_`, takes each node's `.nid`, `.name` and `.conn`, and prints them:

  $ python check_claim.py hw_audio_hda-codec-common.h
  node 1: name=func conn=[]
  node 2: name=dac conn=[]
  node 3: name=out conn=[2]
  node 4: name=adc conn=[5]
  node 5: name=in conn=[]
  CLAIM HOLDS
  EXIT=0

So C1 is true, verbatim: ADC conn is exactly {5}, and the DAC's only consumer is
the line-out pin. Node numbering and stream indices also match the log: node 2
`.stindex = 0`, node 4 `.stindex = 1`.

C2, what `-audiodev none` does. audio/noaudio.c, pinned version:

  static size_t no_write(HWVoiceOut *hw, void *buf, size_t len)
  {
      NoVoiceOut *no = (NoVoiceOut *) hw;
      return audio_rate_get_bytes(&no->rate, &hw->info, len);
  }

  static size_t no_read(HWVoiceIn *hw, void *buf, size_t size)
  {
      NoVoiceIn *no = (NoVoiceIn *) hw;
      int64_t bytes = audio_rate_get_bytes(&no->rate, &hw->info, size);

      audio_pcm_info_clear_buf(&hw->info, buf, bytes / hw->info.bytes_per_frame);
      return bytes;
  }

  $ grep -n "audio_pcm_info_clear_buf" noaudio.c
  99:    audio_pcm_info_clear_buf(&hw->info, buf, bytes / hw->info.bytes_per_frame);

Read plainly: output is consumed at the real-time rate and dropped; input is
zero-filled and delivered at the real-time rate. The substance of C2 is true.
See finding F2.7-F1 for the wording of "0 bytes / all 0x00s".

C5, the framebuffer mode. The kernel does not hardcode the mode; it prints what
the bootloader negotiated, and kernel/limine.conf requests it:

  $ grep -n "resolution" kernel/limine.conf kernel/limine-selftest.conf
  kernel/limine-selftest.conf:13:    resolution: 1024x768x32
  kernel/limine.conf:22:    resolution: 1024x768x32

and a committed CI log shows the negotiated values the log quotes:

  $ grep -rn "framebuffer:" docs/logs/*.log | head -3
  docs/logs/a2-1-ci-41597419124.log:1452:check	Run full check	2026-11-03T07:40:18.2440500Z framebuffer: 1024x768 @ 32bpp, pitch=4096, model=RGB
