P3. Wrong numbers and a bogus path planted in the K5b log itself (tests whether
    any repo tool guards a log's claims). Two edits, then the citation tool:

    $ edit: "supporting all 95 printable ASCII glyphs" -> "... 9 ..."
    $ python tools/check-citations.py > /tmp/cit2.out 2>&1; echo EXIT=$?
    EXIT=1
    (the tool's exit 1 is its pre-existing baseline: "Missing: 20". The edited
     number is not mentioned anywhere in the report.)

    $ edit: "tests/test_font_ref.py" -> "tests/font_ref_test.py"
    $ python tools/check-citations.py; grep does-not-exist
    NOT REPORTED

    The tool scans .log files (tools/check-citations.py:82) but its path
    patterns only accept paths rooted at docs/, tools/, kernel/, mathd/ or
    build/ (tools/check-citations.py:93-96). `tests/...` citations are silently
    outside its reach. A path that does fall inside the pattern set is caught:

    $ edit: "tests/font_ref_test.py" -> "docs/does-not-exist-f2probe.md"
    $ python tools/check-citations.py
      - docs/does-not-exist-f2probe.md          [line 19 of the report]

    So: paths under the five accepted roots are guarded; numbers are never
    guarded; the one path this commit cites is not guarded. Neither edit changes
    any tool's verdict. Reverted:

    $ git checkout -- docs/logs/k5b-audio-roundtrip-analysis.log
    $ git status --short
    ?? .qemu-oracle/

P4 (not a plant, a measurement of the claim's mechanism). The log describes the
    HDA callbacks as calling `AUD_write` / `AUD_read`:

    $ grep -rn "AUD_write\|AUD_read" *.c *.h | wc -l
    0
    $ grep -n "audio_be_write\|audio_be_read" audio_be.c
    102:size_t audio_be_write(AudioBackend *be, SWVoiceOut *sw, void *buf, size_t size)
    113:size_t audio_be_read(AudioBackend *be, SWVoiceIn *sw, void *buf, size_t size)
    $ grep -n "audio_be_read\|audio_be_write" hw_audio_hda-codec.c
    277:        uint32_t read = audio_be_read(st->state->audio_be, st->voice.in,
    357:        uint32_t written = audio_be_write(st->state->audio_be, st->voice.out,

    The callbacks exist (hda-codec.c:265 input, :336 output) and the data flow
