================================================================================
5. FINDINGS
================================================================================

F2.7-F1  (minor, wording)  C2's parenthetical is wrong about capture: audio/
    noaudio.c:99 zero-fills a buffer of the requested length and returns that
    length, so capture delivers silence at the full size. It does not return
    "0 bytes". The parenthetical should say "a zero-filled buffer at the real
    time rate". The conclusion is unaffected.

F2.7-F2  (minor, accuracy)  The log names `AUD_write` and `AUD_read` as the
    functions the HDA callbacks call. Neither exists in QEMU at f71afb4; the
    backend API is `audio_be_write` / `audio_be_read` (audio-be.c:102,113), and
    hw/audio/hda-codec.c uses those. The mechanism described is correct; the
    names are stale. Written by the author as fact, so it counts as a citation
    that does not resolve.

F2.7-F3  (minor, accuracy)  "The backend defaults to `-audiodev none` (or
    dummy)" is imprecise. QEMU builds the default device list from
    audio_prio_list (dbus, spice, then compiled drivers, then "none") and
    `audio_init(NULL)` walks it until one backend initialises; `none` is the
    last fallback, not a default. On a headless runner the practical outcome is
    the one the log states. Same conclusion, wrong mechanism.

F2.7-F4  (process, cross-document conflict)  C4 holds that `-audiodev wav`
    capture "directly violates" the audio-bytes-in-RAM invariant, and on that
    basis treats host capture as unavailable. The lead's later work order A2.15
    (docs/queue-2026-11-03.md) instructs exactly that capture to a CI temp
    directory, with the generated tone deleted afterwards, and commit 3128d7f
    implements it. The two documents disagree, and A2.15's premise (QEMU audio
    is observable from outside the guest) is the one the repo has since acted
    on. K5b's own "stop" decision remains correct for the round trip, which is
    what the work order asked about; the invariant argument in section 2c is
    superseded and should be annotated, not silently left as the record. This is
    the kind of disagreement the lead should settle, not a freebuff edit.

F2.7-F5  (guard gap)  Nothing in the repo checks the numbers in a log. P3
    changed 95 to 9 in this commit's own evidence file and every tool present
    (tools/check-citations.py, scripts/check.sh) stayed in exactly its prior
    state. The glyph claim is protected only because a human re-ran
    tests/test_font_ref.py; the audio analysis has no executable guard at all.
    Also, tools/check-citations.py cannot see `tests/` paths (P3), which is
    where this commit's only cited path lives. Reported for the lead; the tool
    is F3's and the log is A2's.

Not findings, but checked because AGENTS.md asks: cited paths
(`docs/logs/k5b-audio-roundtrip-analysis.log` exists; `tests/test_font_ref.py`
exists; `hw/audio/intel-hda.c`, `hw/audio/hda-codec.c`,
`hw/audio/hda-codec-common.h`, `audio/audio.c` are QEMU paths, not repo paths);
displayed counts (95 glyphs = U+0020..U+007E = 95 entries, re-measured; node
numbers 2 and 4 with stream indices 0 and 1, checked against source); the quoted
work order text matches docs/team.md:1693 line for line, including "If QEMU
cannot give a true round trip, say so and stop."

================================================================================
6. VERDICT ON THE QUESTION ASKED
================================================================================

"Is the commit's claim about QEMU audio true?" Yes. QEMU's duplex codec has no
DAC->ADC connection (verified against QEMU at f71afb4, with a plant showing the
check is sensitive to exactly that property), and under `-audiodev none`
playback is dropped and capture is silence (verified in audio/noaudio.c). A true
round trip therefore needs host-side loopback, exactly as the commit says. The
commit was also entitled to stop: the work order says so in as many words.

Three details in the supporting prose are wrong (F1, F2, F3), one argument is
superseded by a later instruction from the lead (F4), and one guard is missing
(F5). None of them changes the answer, and none of them is something a reviewer
may fix in the author's file.

VERDICT: PASS (with F2.7-F1 to F2.7-F5 recorded for the author and the lead;
F4 needs a lead decision, F5 belongs to the F3 tools)
